# Nexora website — public, non-secret SEO configuration.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (which is git-ignored) and fill in the values when a
# production domain is confirmed. Nothing here is a secret: both values are
# compiled into the public bundle and are readable in the page source.
#
# Nothing is set by default, and that default is deliberate:
#
#   * no confirmed production domain exists yet;
#   * an unconfigured build emits no canonical URL, no `og:url`, no sitemap,
#     and a `noindex` policy — so a development or preview deployment can never
#     advertise a URL it cannot vouch for.
#
# `nexora.example` below is in the reserved `.example` documentation TLD. It is
# a placeholder for the *shape* of the value, not a domain this project owns,
# and it must be replaced before it is ever used.

# The confirmed production origin: https, bare host, no trailing slash, no path.
# Anything else (http, a path, an IP, localhost, a preview host such as
# *.e2b.app) is rejected at build time and leaves canonical URLs disabled.
# VITE_SITE_URL=https://nexora.example

# Enable indexing. Honoured only together with a valid VITE_SITE_URL; on its own
# it is ignored (and logged) so a half-configured launch cannot go live
# half-indexed.
# VITE_SITE_INDEXABLE=true

# ─────────────────────── Developer portal (server only) ──────────────────────
#
# Everything below is read by `server/index.mjs` (the Node process that serves
# the built site and `/api/portal/*`). **None of these is a `VITE_*` variable**,
# so none of them can be inlined into the browser bundle. They must never be
# committed with real values: set them in the hosting environment, or in a local
# `.env` that stays git-ignored and is loaded by your process manager.
#
# Defaults are development-safe. Production refuses to start without a valid
# NEXORA_PORTAL_ORIGIN and secure cookies (see DEVELOPER_PORTAL.md §8).
#
# NEXORA_PORTAL_ENV=production
# NEXORA_PORTAL_ORIGIN=https://<confirmed-production-domain>
# NEXORA_PORTAL_HOST=127.0.0.1
# NEXORA_PORTAL_PORT=4174
# NEXORA_PORTAL_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/nexora-portal
# NEXORA_PORTAL_STATIC_DIR=dist
# NEXORA_PORTAL_SECURE_COOKIES=true
# NEXORA_PORTAL_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=28800
# NEXORA_PORTAL_SESSION_IDLE_SECONDS=1800
# NEXORA_PORTAL_TRUST_PROXY=false
# Point the loader at a file outside the deploy directory (e.g. /etc/nexora-portal.env).
# NEXORA_PORTAL_ENV_FILE=/etc/nexora-portal.env
# ───────────────────── Owner account (server only) ─────────────────────
#
# The Owner / Super Admin account can be provisioned from this file:
#
#   * on startup, the server creates the owner **only** when no owner exists;
#   * an existing owner is never overwritten by a restart;
#   * changing the password requires the explicit command
#       node server/provision-owner.mjs --from-env --rotate-password
#     which also revokes every live session.
#
# These are server-side variables, not `VITE_*` names, so they are never inlined
# into the browser bundle. They are read only by `server/`, and no response, log,
# or error message ever contains the password.
#
# Fill in your own values in a local `.env` (git-ignored). Never commit them, and
# never reuse a test password in production.
#
# NEXORA_PORTAL_OWNER_EMAIL=you@your-domain.example
# NEXORA_PORTAL_OWNER_NAME=Nexora Owner
# NEXORA_PORTAL_OWNER_PASSWORD=<at least 12 characters — generate, do not reuse>
#
# The password may also be typed at a hidden prompt (or piped on stdin) with
# `node server/provision-owner.mjs --email <owner>` instead of being stored in
# this file at all. That command is the fallback when you would rather keep the
# credential out of `.env` entirely.
